    "updated_at": "2022-01-31T22:03:29Z",
    "body": "@dallonf It seems tough to imagine new features being added that facilitate this usage because the “foundation” it would be extending is kind of a mess. With a time machine, I bet sheets associated with elements would end up strictly immutable (only replaceable via child text mutation). Here is my (incomplete, possibly incorrect — def not an expert on this so you’ll want to verify) understanding of what makes the sheet-of-element lifecycle stuff weird:\r\n\r\nFor a style element, the sheet is initialized by parsing the child text content of the element — though it’s is seemingly [not entirely specified](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/2997) how this occurs. Child text content is mutable, so if there are changes to it, [update a style block](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#update-a-style-block) will run again, creating a new sheet (so it’s not 1 element === 1 sheet, I think, even while connected). The sheets themselves are also mutable via the CSSOM API, but mutations effected this way aren’t reflected back to the “source text” child text content.\r\n\r\nThere’s a source of truth problem baked into this which maybe can’t be fixed web-compatibly. I think there are also more possible states in play than may initially be apparent and it’s not clear how “Shadow DOM should be considered a document for the purposes of this process” would map to them.\r\n\r\n~Fortunately, it looks like the early restriction on mutation of constructable stylesheets [may end up lifted](https://github.com/WICG/construct-stylesheets/issues/119). This seems right to me because there is no “competing” source of truth for these stylesheets.~ Per @jogibear9988 (next comment), a constraint on insertRule with constructable style sheets _already_ doesn’t exist (and probably never did?).",
